1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,870
It's night.

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:02,630
You're flying a fighter jet at around

3
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:04,990
two hundred and forty kilometres an hour.

4
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:08,010
Somewhere down there in
the dark is your runway.

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,090
It's moving.

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:10,910
It's rising and falling with the waves.

7
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:12,810
And the patch of deck you have to hit is

8
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:15,270
smaller than almost any runway on land.

9
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:17,390
You can't slow down to land.

10
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,030
You can't stop on your own.

11
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:20,710
Something has to grab your plane and

12
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:23,230
stop it for you, in about two seconds.

13
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,430
And the moment your wheels hit the deck,

14
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,850
you do something that
sounds completely crazy.

15
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:30,030
You push the engines to full power.

16
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:30,650
Why?

17
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:32,570
To answer that, we need to understand

18
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:35,230
how an aircraft carrier really works.

19
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,370
The biggest aircraft carriers in
the world belong to the United

20
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:43,850
States Navy: the Nimitz class, and
the newest one, the Gerald R. Ford.

21
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,030
A Nimitz carrier is about three
hundred and thirty metres long.

22
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,810
Stand it on its end, and it's
about as tall as the Eiffel Tower.

23
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:55,610
Fully loaded, it weighs around
a hundred thousand tonnes,

24
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:58,330
and it can still sail at
more than thirty knots.

25
00:00:58,580 --> 00:00:59,850
With its air wing on board,

26
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,190
around five thousand people live
and work on a Nimitz carrier.

27
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:05,290
That's more than many small towns.

28
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:06,730
And it doesn't sail alone.

29
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:08,910
Cruisers and destroyers
guard it against missiles

30
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,990
and submarines, and supply
ships keep it stocked.

31
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,610
Together they're called
a carrier strike group,

32
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:17,170
and the escorts bring
thousands more people.

33
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,890
But the whole point of
this ship is its aircraft.

34
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:23,070
An air wing usually has more
than forty strike fighters,

35
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:27,930
like the F/A-18 Super Hornet and
the F-35C, plus the specialists:

36
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:32,070
the E-2D Hawkeye with its giant
radar dish, the EA-18G Growler

37
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,090
that jams enemy radar, helicopters
for submarine hunting and rescue,

38
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:39,810
and transport aircraft that
bring people, mail and parts.

39
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:43,390
The exact mix changes from ship
to ship and mission to mission.

40
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:46,310
But every one of those
aircraft has the same problem.

41
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,350
This runway is far too short.

42
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:52,810
On land, a fighter jet often uses
well over a kilometre of runway.

43
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:56,570
On a Nimitz carrier, the catapult
gives it about ninety metres.

44
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,850
So the carrier throws it into the air.

45
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,910
Under the deck runs a long track.

46
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:02,690
The jet's nose wheel is attached to

47
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:04,470
a shuttle that rides in that track.

48
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:06,970
The pilot pushes the
engines to full power,

49
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:10,710
but the jet doesn't move, because
a holdback is holding it in place.

50
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:13,830
Behind it, a steel wall
rises out of the deck.

51
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:17,770
It's a jet blast deflector,
and it stops the scorching

52
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,370
exhaust from hitting the
people and planes behind.

53
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:22,310
The deck crew check everything.

54
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:26,330
Then the catapult officer, nicknamed
the shooter, gives the signal.

55
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:29,010
The holdback lets go,
and the catapult fires.

56
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,570
In about two seconds, the
jet goes from standing

57
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:34,510
still to flying speed,
around two hundred and

58
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:37,130
eighty kilometres an hour
for a typical fighter.

59
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:39,810
On Nimitz carriers, that
push comes from steam,

60
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:41,510
made with heat from the ship's reactors.

61
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:44,590
The Ford uses an electric
system called EMALS.

62
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:47,650
It stores energy in big spinning
rotors between launches,

63
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:50,990
then releases it through
electronic power converters into a

64
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:54,310
long electric motor that pulls
the shuttle down the track.

65
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:57,690
It's easier to fine-tune for
heavy jets and light drones alike.

66
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:59,530
The carrier usually helps too.

67
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:01,230
It steers into the wind,

68
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:03,910
so more air is already
flowing over the wings.

69
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:05,370
Launching is violent.

70
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:08,590
But bringing that jet back is even harder.

71
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:10,750
Let's go back to our night landing.

72
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:13,630
To stay on the right path
down, the pilot watches

73
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:16,330
a light on the side of the
deck called the meatball.

74
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:18,330
If the ball of light sits level with a row

75
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:21,050
of green lights, you're on the glide path.

76
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:22,570
Too high, and it rises.

77
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:24,370
Too low, and it sinks.

78
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:27,530
Beside the landing area stands
a landing signal officer,

79
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:32,690
an experienced pilot who watches every
approach and talks the pilots in by radio.

80
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,890
Across the landing area
are thick steel cables,

81
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:38,050
raised a little above the deck.

82
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,170
Most Nimitz-class carriers have four.

83
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:43,710
The Ford, and the two newest
Nimitz ships, have three.

84
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:46,150
Under the tail of the jet hangs a hook.

85
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,210
The pilot flies the plane firmly onto the

86
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:50,670
deck so the hook can
catch one of those wires.

87
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:53,490
On a four-wire deck,
pilots aim for the third,

88
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:56,850
because it gives the safest margin
from the back edge of the ship.

89
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,250
Then the wire pays out,
pulling against huge

90
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,910
machines below the deck that
soak up the jet's energy.

91
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:04,710
The system is built to stop
a jet weighing more than

92
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:07,518
twenty tonnes, coming in
at around two hundred and

93
00:04:07,568 --> 00:04:10,630
forty kilometres an hour,
in roughly a hundred metres.

94
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:12,190
In about two seconds.

95
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:15,450
Those cables take a terrible
beating, so the crew inspect them

96
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,870
constantly and replace them whenever
wear and maintenance rules say so.

97
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:23,890
So, why push to full power
at the exact moment you land?

98
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,430
Because sometimes the
hook misses every wire.

99
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:28,090
That's called a bolter.

100
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:29,750
If the engines were at low power,

101
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,910
the jet could roll off the
end of the deck into the sea.

102
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:35,450
At full power, it simply takes off again,

103
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,390
flies around, and tries one more time.

104
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,850
That's also why the
landing area is angled,

105
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,190
about nine degrees off to the side.

106
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,530
A jet that misses flies off past the bow,

107
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,710
instead of crashing into the
planes parked at the front.

108
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:53,090
And if a jet can't land normally
at all, say its hook is damaged,

109
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:55,990
the crew can raise a
giant net across the deck,

110
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,850
called the barricade, and
catch the plane in it.

111
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,850
All of this happens on
a deck packed with jets,

112
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:03,430
spinning engines and fuel.

113
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,230
So how do they keep people safe?

114
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:06,310
With colours.

115
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:07,850
Everyone on the flight deck wears a

116
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:09,910
coloured jersey that shows their job.

117
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,670
Yellow are the officers and directors
who control and move the aircraft,

118
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,290
including the catapult and
arresting-gear officers.

119
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:21,210
Green run and maintain the
catapults and arresting gear,

120
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:23,730
along with many aircraft
maintenance crews.

121
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:25,630
Purple handle the fuel.

122
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:27,390
Everyone calls them grapes.

123
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:30,970
Red handle bombs and missiles,
and crash and salvage.

124
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:33,410
Blue chain the jets
down, chock the wheels,

125
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,910
and drive the tractors and elevators.

126
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,870
Brown are the plane captains,

127
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:40,510
each responsible for
getting one jet ready.

128
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:44,350
And white covers several safety
jobs: landing signal officers,

129
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:47,430
safety observers, inspectors
and medical teams.

130
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,230
At a glance, anyone can
see who's doing what.

131
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:53,130
High up on the island,
the tower beside the deck,

132
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:54,870
is primary flight control.

133
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,130
That's where the Air
Boss runs every aircraft

134
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,770
on the deck and in the
air close to the ship.

135
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,090
During busy flying,

136
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:04,330
launches and landings often
run in waves called cycles.

137
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:07,090
A common rhythm is roughly
every hour and a half,

138
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:10,750
with a dozen or more aircraft in
each, but it changes with the mission.

139
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,950
A busy airport, squeezed onto a
moving deck, in the middle of the sea.

140
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:17,190
The Navy didn't learn
all of this on paper.

141
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:21,850
On the twenty-ninth of July,
nineteen sixty-seven, the carrier USS

142
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:26,090
Forrestal was preparing to launch
aircraft off the coast of Vietnam.

143
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:29,650
On the crowded deck, an
electrical malfunction

144
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:33,190
fired a Zuni rocket from
under an F-4B Phantom.

145
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:38,030
It struck the external fuel tank
of an A-4E Skyhawk across the deck.

146
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:40,110
Fuel poured out and caught fire,

147
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:42,790
and bombs on the burning
aircraft began to explode.

148
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:46,210
One of the first men to
run toward the fire was

149
00:06:46,260 --> 00:06:49,590
Chief Gerald Farrier, with
a portable extinguisher.

150
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,390
He was killed in the first explosion.

151
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:54,930
One hundred and thirty-four
men were killed.

152
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:57,110
One hundred and sixty-one were injured.

153
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,950
Twenty-one aircraft were destroyed.

154
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:00,610
The fire changed the Navy.

155
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,750
It brought back firefighting
training for every sailor,

156
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:07,970
not just specialist teams, and reshaped
how carriers handle fires on deck.

157
00:07:08,220 --> 00:07:10,250
Today, sailors train at a firefighting

158
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,310
school named after Chief Farrier.

159
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:14,470
On a ship, there's no fire brigade coming.

160
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,210
The fire brigade is everyone on board.

161
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:18,620
Back to the flying.

162
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:21,330
Not every aircraft fits on
the flight deck at once.

163
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:24,190
Below it is a huge garage
called the hangar bay,

164
00:07:24,460 --> 00:07:26,130
where jets are repaired and stored.

165
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:27,870
To move jets between the two,

166
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:30,650
there are giant elevators
on the sides of the ship.

167
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:33,650
A Nimitz carrier has four,
and each one can lift a

168
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:36,270
fighter from the hangar to
the flight deck in seconds.

169
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,870
Separate weapons elevators bring bombs and

170
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:41,950
missiles up from magazines
deep inside the hull.

171
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:45,170
So what powers the
catapults, the elevators,

172
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:47,530
and a town of five thousand people?

173
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:49,050
Two nuclear reactors.

174
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:50,690
They heat water into steam.

175
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:53,130
The steam spins turbines
that turn the propellers

176
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:55,630
and make electricity for the whole ship.

177
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,570
A Nimitz carrier runs for more than

178
00:07:57,620 --> 00:08:00,070
twenty years on its first load of fuel.

179
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:02,870
Halfway through its life,
it gets refuelled once,

180
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,850
in a huge overhaul that
takes several years.

181
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,850
The Ford's newer reactors
generate much more electricity.

182
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:12,830
The Navy says nearly three times
the capacity of earlier carriers.

183
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:15,410
That's what makes the
electric catapults possible.

184
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:18,930
Nuclear power means the carrier
never stops for its own fuel.

185
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:22,010
But the jets still burn
jet fuel, and lots of it.

186
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:24,790
So supply ships regularly
come alongside at sea,

187
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:29,290
pumping over aviation fuel and passing
across food, parts and ammunition.

188
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:30,970
Now think about the people who keep those

189
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:33,670
flights going, far out at sea for months.

190
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,750
A Nimitz carrier's galleys can serve

191
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:37,890
around eighteen thousand meals a day.

192
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:38,930
The water?

193
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:41,103
Distillers turn seawater into about four

194
00:08:41,153 --> 00:08:43,470
hundred thousand gallons
of fresh water a day.

195
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:46,070
That's around one and
a half million litres.

196
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:49,430
Most sailors sleep in stacked
bunks, in rooms shared with

197
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:52,930
dozens of others, with a small
locker for everything they own.

198
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:56,370
There are gyms, a post
office, a medical department

199
00:08:56,420 --> 00:08:59,410
with an operating room,
and even a barber shop.

200
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:03,270
The newest carrier, the USS Gerald
R. Ford, was commissioned in

201
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:07,370
twenty seventeen, at a cost of
about thirteen billion dollars.

202
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:10,570
It has the electric
catapults, new arresting gear,

203
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:12,450
three elevators instead of four,

204
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:15,790
and a smaller island moved further
back to free up deck space.

205
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:18,710
All of it is meant to launch
and recover more aircraft.

206
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:22,210
The Navy's design requirement is a
hundred and sixty flights a day for

207
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:26,250
weeks at a time, and up to two
hundred and seventy in a short surge.

208
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:29,730
Those are targets, not everyday
numbers, and the Pentagon's

209
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:32,750
own testers have questioned
whether the ship can reach them.

210
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:35,430
So let's land that jet one more time.

211
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:37,290
The ship has turned into the wind.

212
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,870
You keep the meatball level
with the green lights.

213
00:09:40,020 --> 00:09:42,870
The landing signal officer
says you're looking good.

214
00:09:43,020 --> 00:09:44,390
The wheels slam down.

215
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:46,350
You push to full power, just in case.

216
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:48,350
Your hook catches the third wire.

217
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:51,430
And in about two seconds,
your jet is standing still,

218
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:54,590
on a floating runway, in
the middle of the ocean.

219
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:57,730
That's how an aircraft carrier
works: a floating airport,

220
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:01,510
a power station and a
town, built around one job.

221
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:04,490
Throw jets into the sky,
and catch them again.

222
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:07,690
If you want to see what this
ship costs compared to every

223
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:10,630
other kind of ship, from a
small fishing boat to the

224
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:14,290
thirteen-billion-dollar Ford,
that video is right here.
